{"name":"Blued Steel","hex":"#535a61","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blued","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of blue"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the property of having been chemically passivated."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blued"},{"term":"steel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An artificial metal produced from iron, harder and more elastic than elemental iron; used figuratively as a symbol of hardness.","Any item made of this metal, particularly including:\nBladed or pointed weapons, as swords, javelins, daggers."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of steel.","Similar to steel in color, strength, or the like; steely."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/steel"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bluing (steel)","blocks":[{"text":"Bluing, sometimes spelled as blueing, is a passivation process in which steel is partially protected against rust using a black oxide coating. It is named after the blue-black appearance of the resulting protective finish. Bluing involves an electrochemical conversion coating resulting from an oxidizing chemical reaction with iron on the surface selectively forming magnetite, the black oxide of iron. In comparison, rust, the red oxide of iron, undergoes an extremely large volume change upon hydration; as a result, the oxide easily flakes off, causing the typical reddish rusting away of iron. Black oxide provides minimal protection against corrosion, unless also treated with a water-displacing oil to reduce wetting and galvanic action. In colloquial use, thin coatings of black oxide are often termed \"gun bluing\", while heavier coatings are termed \"black oxide\". Both refer to the same chemical process for providing true gun bluing."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Button_%28France%29%2C_19th_century_%28CH_18322949%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Button_%28France%29%2C_19th_century_%28CH_18322949%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bluing_(steel)","wikidata":"Q548465"},{"title":"Steel","blocks":[{"text":"Steel is an alloy of iron and carbon that demonstrates improved mechanical properties compared to the pure form of iron. Due to its high elastic modulus, yield strength, fracture strength and low raw material cost, steel is one of the most commonly manufactured materials in the world. Steel is used in structures, in bridges, infrastructure, tools, ships, trains, cars, bicycles, machines, electrical appliances, furniture, and weapons."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/%D0%90%D1%80%D0%BC%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%83%D1%80%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%82.jpg/330px-%D0%90%D1%80%D0%BC%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%83%D1%80%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%82.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steel","wikidata":"Q11427"},{"title":"Steel blue","blocks":[{"text":"Steel blue is a shade of blue color that resembles blue steel, i.e., steel which has been subjected to bluing for protection from rust. It is one of the less vibrant shades of blue, and is usually identified as a blue-gray color. An archaic term for it is chalybeous."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Niter_and_Color_Case.jpg/330px-Niter_and_Color_Case.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steel_blue","wikidata":"Q1003676"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/M1935A-RustBlue-03.jpg/500px-M1935A-RustBlue-03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/M1935A-RustBlue-03.jpg/500px-M1935A-RustBlue-03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Acid solution applied to bare metal","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AM1935A-RustBlue-03.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/M1935A-RustBlue-AfterBoil-01.jpg/500px-M1935A-RustBlue-AfterBoil-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/M1935A-RustBlue-AfterBoil-01.jpg/500px-M1935A-RustBlue-AfterBoil-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"After boiling rusted parts","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AM1935A-RustBlue-AfterBoil-01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/M1935A-RustBlue-FirstOiling-01.jpg/500px-M1935A-RustBlue-FirstOiling-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/M1935A-RustBlue-FirstOiling-01.jpg/500px-M1935A-RustBlue-FirstOiling-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"After eight rust, carding and oiling sessions","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AM1935A-RustBlue-FirstOiling-01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/7%2C62x54R_carbine_SVT-O_at_ARMS_%26_Hunting_2015.jpg/500px-7%2C62x54R_carbine_SVT-O_at_ARMS_%26_Hunting_2015.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/7%2C62x54R_carbine_SVT-O_at_ARMS_%26_Hunting_2015.jpg/1280px-7%2C62x54R_carbine_SVT-O_at_ARMS_%26_Hunting_2015.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/7%2C62x54R_carbine_SVT-O_at_ARMS_%26_Hunting_2015.jpg/500px-7%2C62x54R_carbine_SVT-O_at_ARMS_%26_Hunting_2015.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Plumbing is common on Soviet era firearms. Note the plumb brown colored bolt of the SVT rifle. The plum was the result of higher chromium content in the bluing bath, as well as temperature/time duration. These were being refurbed en masse so these big bluing baths were basically running 24/7 and the ratios of chemicals was constantly changing as rifles were being blued and as workers were replenishing the bluing baths.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A7%2C62x54R_carbine_SVT-O_at_ARMS_%26_Hunting_2015.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Konstruktionsst%C3%A5l.JPG/500px-Konstruktionsst%C3%A5l.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Konstruktionsst%C3%A5l.JPG/1280px-Konstruktionsst%C3%A5l.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Konstruktionsst%C3%A5l.JPG/500px-Konstruktionsst%C3%A5l.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Structural steel bars of different shapes used in construction","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKonstruktionsst%C3%A5l.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Steel_wire_rope.JPG/500px-Steel_wire_rope.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Steel_wire_rope.JPG/1280px-Steel_wire_rope.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Steel_wire_rope.JPG/500px-Steel_wire_rope.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Steel cable of a colliery headframe","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASteel_wire_rope.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/